c-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:530:| `Readable stream does not buffer entire archive in memory` | Streaming invariant (measure peak memory during large input) |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:541:| `cumulative byte counter rejects oversize archives` | Memory DoS defense |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:754:16. Trigger an export on a fully-exportable package. Confirm the response includes a download link, download succeeds, and the `.mpkz` archive contains `_nav.md` + one entry per member.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/tech-design.md:69:archive design, but Epic 1 stays request/response only.\\n./tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts:218:    processHistoryItemsByProcessId: {\\n./tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts:1221:      processHistoryItemsByProcessId: {\\n./tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts:1326:      processHistoryItemsByProcessId: {\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/epic.md:26:archive browsing, external-source attachment, or full GitHub workflow\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/epic.md:44:materials remain reference-based. Full archive, turn, chunk, and derived-view\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/epic.md:128:- Full-fidelity archive, turn derivation, chunk derivation, or archive browsing\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/epic.md:153:| A9 | Epic 6 implements the repository-focused source-management half of PRD Feature 5 and Epic 7 will implement the archive and derived-view half | Validated | Platform | The Feature 5 implementation split is intentional, and external/MCP source attachment remains a later follow-on beyond the current sequence |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/epic.md:166:| Feature 5 overview | Feature 5 now states that implementation may land as more than one epic, with repository-focused source-management first and archive/derived-view work later | This epic intentionally covers the repository-focused source-management half of Feature 5 while Epic 7 covers the archive half |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/epic.md:759:- Epic 7 for full archive, turn, chunk, and derived-view behavior\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/epic.md:945:- [ ] Artifact-model alignment (Epic 5), archive work (Epic 7), external-source attachment, and broader GitHub workflow management are explicitly deferred or out of scope\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:227:function readTarEntry(archive: Buffer, entryPath: string): string | null {\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:230:  while (offset + 512 <= archive.byteLength) {\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:231:    const header = archive.subarray(offset, offset + 512);\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:243:    const content = archive.subarray(offset, offset + size);\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:257:  it('TC-5.1a and TC-5.2 export the current package as a pinned .mpkz archive with manifest metadata', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:313:    const archiveBytes =\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:317:    const archive = gunzipSync(archiveBytes);\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:318:    const manifest = readTarEntry(archive, '_nav.md');\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:319:    const specification = readTarEntry(archive, '1-feature-specification.md');\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:347:  it('preserves package members with colliding display names as distinct archive paths', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:383:    const archiveBytes =\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:387:    const archive = gunzipSync(archiveBytes);\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:388:    const manifest = readTarEntry(archive, '_nav.md');\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:389:    const firstSpec = readTarEntry(archive, '1-spec.md');\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:390:    const secondSpec = readTarEntry(archive, '2-spec.md');\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:9:Epic 4 adds three new server concerns beneath the existing surface set: a dedicated review route family, a server-side markdown render pipeline, and a streaming export path that composes Convex File Storage content into a `.mpkz` archive. None of these are public products of their own — they are deeper implementation layers inside the existing authenticated application boundary.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:148:| `export.service.ts` | NEW | Two-phase export: POST returns `ExportPackageResponse` JSON with a signed download URL; GET at that URL (handled by the same `routes/review.ts` module) streams the `.mpkz` archive through `createPackageFromEntries` into the Fastify reply body | `PlatformStore`, `@liminal-build/markdown-package`, `export-url-signing` | AC-5 |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:604:stay exportable because the archive packages pinned bytes, not the rendered\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:783:  // Phase 2: GET /exports/:exportId?token=... — verify token, stream the .mpkz archive\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1134:Export is a two-phase flow. Phase 1 is a POST that validates the export request, mints a signed download URL, and returns metadata. Phase 2 is a GET at the signed URL that streams the `.mpkz` archive bytes through `@liminal-build/markdown-package`'s `createPackageFromEntries` into the Fastify reply body. Splitting the two phases matches the spec's `ExportPackageResponse` contract (with `downloadUrl` + `expiresAt`), lets the client render a real download link before bytes flow, and gives expired-URL handling (AC-5.3b) a natural implementation.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1206:- **Member filenames.** Each member's archive filename is derived from its `displayName`. Markdown members use `.md`; unsupported-but-durable members still export their pinned bytes under a stable fallback filename rather than being filtered out. Collisions are resolved by appending `-{position}` to later duplicates.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1208:- **Download URL reuse semantics.** The signed URL is **reusable within the expiry window** — the same URL can be GET'd multiple times until `expiresAt`, each GET re-validates the token, re-checks access + exportability, and streams a fresh archive. No single-use enforcement. This lets users copy the download link, re-download after an interrupted transfer, or share within the expiry if they choose. After `expiresAt`, every GET returns 404 `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND` regardless of how many prior downloads succeeded.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1210:- **Mid-stream failures** (e.g., Convex File Storage fetch fails for one member) tear down the HTTP response; the client sees a truncated download and re-exports. We do not attempt to recover a partial archive.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1233:| TC-5.1a (phase 2) | export download streams valid tar.gz | `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | POST result's downloadUrl | GET returns 200 `application/gzip`; stream extracts to valid archive with expected members |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1236:| TC-5.2a | export matches reviewed versions | `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | package pinned at V1; V2 also exists | extracted archive contains V1 bytes (not V2) |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1237:| TC-5.2b | export manifest | `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | exported archive | extracted `_nav.md` contains package identity + member identities in package order |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1402:The returned `Readable` stream emits `.mpkz` (or `.mpk` if `compress: false`) bytes. Callers pipe it into a Fastify reply, a file system write stream, or any other consumer. The `manifest` is written as the first tar entry (`_nav.md`), followed by one entry per yielded `PackageEntry`. The `maxEntryBytes` and `maxTotalBytes` caps enforce bounded memory use per entry and bounded archive size overall; exceeding either terminates the stream with a `PackageError`.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1442:1. **Decompression bomb protection**: every `createGunzip()` invocation passes `{ maxOutputLength: <cap> }`. The cap defaults to 256 MB per archive; `createPackageFromEntries` also enforces a per-entry `maxEntryBytes` cap (default 64 MB) inside its entry-processing loop.\\n./apps/platform/server/schemas/archive.ts:2:  archiveDerivedViewRefreshRequestSchema,\\n./apps/platform/server/schemas/archive.ts:3:  archivePageSchema,\\n./apps/platform/server/schemas/archive.ts:4:  archivePaginationQuerySchema,\\n./apps/platform/server/schemas/archive.ts:5:  archiveRouteParamsSchema,\\n./apps/platform/server/schemas/archive.