al-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:293:| TC-6.1b | `tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts` | `TC-6.1b reopens process source attachment state` | GET process work surface returns current sources |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:296:| TC-6.3a | `tests/service/server/projects-api.test.ts` | `TC-6.3a one failing source does not hide healthy sources` | Section returns healthy rows plus bounded error/degraded row |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:424:        evidence: \"The story/design says project-scoped actions appear in the project source section and Story 3 includes refresh/rehydration for `stale` and `not_hydrated` attachments. In the client, `renderSourceAttachmentSection()` renders refresh controls for any source whose hydration state is `stale` or `not_hydrated` regardless of scope (`apps/platform/client/features/projects/source-attachment-section.ts`). In the new runtime path, `RuntimeSourceHydrationExecutor.resolveTargetProcessId()` returns `null` for project-scoped attachments unless exactly one current process references the source, and `refreshRecoverableSource()` then returns `kind: 'not_available'` with message `no single current process working copy owns this source attachment` (`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts`). The modified API tests only verify successful refresh for process-scoped attachments; no server or client test proves the project-scoped case or suppresses the project-shell control when the backend will 409. This leaves project-scoped recoverability behavior unresolved and can present a false recovery path on the project shell.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:441:        evidence: \"The story/design says project-scoped actions appear in the project source section and Story 3 includes refresh/rehydration for `stale` and `not_hydrated` attachments. In the client, `renderSourceAttachmentSection()` renders refresh controls for any source whose hydration state is `stale` or `not_hydrated` regardless of scope (`apps/platform/client/features/projects/source-attachment-section.ts`). In the new runtime path, `RuntimeSourceHydrationExecutor.resolveTargetProcessId()` returns `null` for project-scoped attachments unless exactly one current process references the source, and `refreshRecoverableSource()` then returns `kind: 'not_available'` with message `no single current process working copy owns this source attachment` (`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts`). The modified API tests only verify successful refresh for process-scoped attachments; no server or client test proves the project-scoped case or suppresses the project-shell control when the backend will 409. This leaves project-scoped recoverability behavior unresolved and can present a false recovery path on the project shell.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:477:      - \"Production refresh now has a new scope ambiguity for project-scoped attachments: the backend requires exactly one current owning process, while the project-shell UI still offers refresh purely from `stale`/`not_hydrated` state, so some project-scoped rows can advertise a recovery path that immediately returns `SOURCE_ATTACHMENT_REFRESH_NOT_AVAILABLE`.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:697:        evidence: \"`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts` defines `ImmediateSourceHydrationExecutor`, which always returns `{ kind: 'settled' }` with a fresh timestamp. `apps/platform/server/app.ts` constructs `DefaultSourceRefreshService` without supplying any other executor, so production always uses that stub. On every settled result, `refreshSource()` persists `hydrationState: 'hydrated'`, clears `freshnessReason`, and stamps `lastHydratedResolvedRef`/`lastObservedRemoteResolvedRef` from GitHub even though no working-copy hydration or validation has happened. The only `working_copy_missing` references outside tests are a fixture and a condition check in `deriveHydrationState`, so the real app cannot detect or surface that recoverable stale case, and the pending/failed branches are only exercised through test doubles/fixtures rather than the runtime path.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:713:        evidence: \"`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts` defines `ImmediateSourceHydrationExecutor`, which always returns `{ kind: 'settled' }` with a fresh timestamp. `apps/platform/server/app.ts` constructs `DefaultSourceRefreshService` without supplying any other executor, so production always uses that stub. On every settled result, `refreshSource()` persists `hydrationState: 'hydrated'`, clears `freshnessReason`, and stamps `lastHydratedResolvedRef`/`lastObservedRemoteResolvedRef` from GitHub even though no working-copy hydration or validation has happened. The only `working_copy_missing` references outside tests are a fixture and a condition check in `deriveHydrationState`, so the real app cannot detect or surface that recoverable stale case, and the pending/failed branches are only exercised through test doubles/fixtures rather than the runtime path.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:792:    planSummary: \"Replaced the fake immediate-refresh success path with a runtime-backed hydration executor that resolves a real process working-copy target, uses the provider adapter hydration/rehydration seam, returns pending/failed/not-available truthfully, and only marks a source hydrated after actual runtime refresh completion.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-006.md:835:      - \"Project-scoped refresh now requires exactly one current process working set to own the source attachment; if zero or multiple current processes reference it, the route returns SOURCE_ATTACHMENT_REFRESH_NOT_AVAILABLE because Story 3 does not define a cross-process hydration target.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/chunk-3-verification-codex.md:9:| 3. Real Octokit code checkpoint writer | SATISFIED | `apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/code-checkpoint-writer.ts:1-309`; `apps/platform/server/app.ts:158-165`; `apps/platform/server/config.ts:32-38`; `apps/platform/server/index.ts:48-54`; `package.json:37-39`; `pnpm-lock.yaml:9-13,656-658,2054-2059`; `tests/integration/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer-integration.test.ts:93-265` | Real writer exists, uses `@octokit/rest@22.0.1`, authenticates from `GITHUB_TOKEN`, writes via `repos.createOrUpdateFileContents`, invents no branches or PRs, and returns structured failures for auth/conflict/network paths. Production `createApp` defaults to `OctokitCodeCheckpointWriter`. I ran the dedicated GitHub integration test with verbose output and observed live GitHub `GET`/`PUT` traffic; it passed 3/3 tests. Cleanup/isolation is good in practice, but slightly weaker than requested because it uses suite-level `afterAll` rather than per-test `try/finally`. |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/chunk-3-verification-codex.md:46:- Convex projection returns it on every source summary: `convex/sourceAttachments.ts:52-63`\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls/sonnet-review-round-2.md:36:| TC-1.1a | Environment state visible on first load | `process-work-surface-api.test.ts`: \"TC-1.1a bootstrap returns environment state on first load\" | SATISFIED |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls/sonnet-review-round-2.md:37:| TC-1.1b | No active environment renders legible state | `process-work-surface-api.test.ts`: \"TC-1.1b bootstrap returns explicit absent environment state for an early process\"; `process-work-surface-page.test.ts`: \"TC-1.1b absent environment still renders a legible state\" | SATISFIED |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls/sonnet-review-round-2.md:42:| TC-1.1g | `stale` — rehydrate enabled, rebuild explained | `process-controls.test.ts`: \"TC-1.1g stale state enables rehydrate and explains unavailable rebuild path\"; `process-work-surface-api.test.ts`: \"TC-1.1g bootstrap returns stale environment truth from durable state\"; `process-foundation-contracts.test.ts`: stale recovery control derivation | SATISFIED |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls/sonnet-review-round-2.md:46:| TC-1.1k | `unavailable` — controls visible, environment actions explained | `process-controls.test.ts`: \"TC-1.1k unavailable state keeps controls visible and explains blocked environment actions\"; `process-work-surface-api.test.ts`: \"returns environment unavailable instead of collapsing the whole surface when the environment read fails\"; `process-work-surface-page.test.ts`: \"renders unavailable environment state without hiding the durable process surface\" | SATISFIED |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls/sonnet-review-round-2.md:93:- Added unlabeled \"bootstrap returns latest checkpoint visibility\" and \"returns environment unavailable instead of collapsing the whole surface\" tests\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls/sonnet-review-round-2.md:157:3. **TC-1.1i (`failed`) — both `rehydrate` and `rebuild` enabled simultaneously.** The `resolveRebuildControlState` returns `enabledState()` for any `failed` environment. The `resolveRehydrateControlState` returns `enabledState()` for `failed` when `environmentId !== null`. This means both are enabled when the environment has a recoverable ID, which is permitted by the spec (\"become enabled only when that recovery path is valid\"). The fixture reflects this. No contradiction, but the path where the environment is failed with a null environmentId (rehydrate disabled, rebuild enabled) is implicitly tested through the `lostEnvironmentProcessControlsFixture` rather than a dedicated `failed-without-environmentId` fixture. Minor coverage gap.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/epic-review-codex.json:114:      \"assessment\": \"ConvexPlatformStore.hasCanonicalRecoveryMaterials() always returns true, so missing-prerequisite rejection is not real on the live Convex path.\",\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/stories/05-rehydrate-rebuild-and-recovery.md:80:  - Then: The process returns to a usable environment state without requiring the prior working copy to survive\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/stories/05-rehydrate-rebuild-and-recovery.md:145:Once one of these actions returns success, later preparation, hydration, recovery, or checkpoint failures surface as visible environment or checkpoint failure states in the same process surface session and on later reopen. Those later failures do not retroactively replace an already-accepted response with an HTTP error.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/stories/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls.md:118:| Get process work surface | `GET` | `/api/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}` | Returns the process work surface, including environment summary and stable control states |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/epic-review-codex.md:12:- The execution contract is materially thinner than the approved tech design. The design expects execution to return process status, history items, output writes, side-work writes, and checkpoint candidates; the implementation only returns `succeeded|failed` plus timestamps, and the orchestration layer only flips environment states. Evidence: `apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/provider-adapter.ts:15-23`, `apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/process-environment.service.ts:359-443`, `docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/tech-design-server.md:564-570`.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/epic-review-codex.md:22:| Real Convex canonical-material recovery query | STUBBED (KNOWN) | `ConvexPlatformStore.hasCanonicalRecoveryMaterials()` always returns `true`, so missing-prerequisite rejection is not real on the live Convex path. Evidence: `apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts:876-878`. |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/epic-review-codex.md:70:| AC-3.3 | UNRESOLVED | `checkpointing` is real, but the integrated execution path cannot organically transition to `waiting` because the execution contract only returns `succeeded|failed`. Evidence: `apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/provider-adapter.ts:15-23`, `docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/epic.md:319-329`. |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:262:| TC-1.1a | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-1.1a creates a project-scoped source attachment` | POST project route returns 201 summary |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:268:| TC-1.4a | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-1.4a rejects invalid repository identity without partial row` | Mock resolver returns invalid; store unchanged |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:269:| TC-1.4b | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-1.4b rejects inaccessible repository without partial row` | Mock resolver returns inaccessible; 503 |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:271:| TC-2.2a | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-2.2a updates source metadata` | PATCH route returns updated summary |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:279:| TC-3.3a | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-3.3a refresh updates one source in place` | Response returns settled source summary |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:281:| TC-4.1a | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-4.1a returns informing source provenance` | GET process provenance includes `informed_work` |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:283:| TC-4.2a | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-4.2a returns receiving source provenance` | GET process provenance includes `received_code_update` |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:287:| TC-5.1a | `tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts` | `TC-5.1a detaches project-scoped source` | DELETE returns detached response |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:292:| TC-6.1a | `tests/service/server/projects-api.test.ts` | `TC-6.1a reopens project source attachment state` | GET project shell returns source state after reload |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:293:| TC-6.1b | `tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts` | `TC-6.1b reopens process source attachment state` | GET process work surface returns current sources |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:296:| TC-6.3a | `tests/service/server/projects-api.test.ts` | `TC-6.3a one failing source does not hide healthy sources` | Section returns healthy rows plus bounded error/degraded row |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:668:        evidence: \"`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts` defines `ImmediateSourceHydrationExecutor`, which always returns `{ kind: 'settled' }` with a fresh timestamp. `apps/platform/server/app.ts` constructs `DefaultSourceRefreshService` without supplying any other executor, so production always uses that stub. On every settled result, `refreshSource()` persists `hydrationState: 'hydrated'`, clears `freshnessReason`, and stamps `lastHydratedResolvedRef`/`lastObservedRemoteResolvedRef` from GitHub even though no working-copy hydration or validation has happened. The only `working_copy_missing` references outside tests are a fixture and a condition check in `deriveHydrationState`, so the real app cannot detect or surface that recoverable stale case, and the pending/failed branches are only exercised through test doubles/fixtures rather than the runtime path.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:684:        evidence: \"`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts` defines `ImmediateSourceHydrationExecutor`, which always returns `{ kind: 'settled' }` with a fresh timestamp. `apps/platform/server/app.ts` constructs `DefaultSourceRefreshService` without supplying any other executor, so production always uses that stub. On every settled result, `refreshSource()` persists `hydrationState: 'hydrated'`, clears `freshnessReason`, and stamps `lastHydratedResolvedRef`/`lastObservedRemoteResolvedRef` from GitHub even though no working-copy hydration or validation has happened. The only `working_copy_missing` references outside tests are a fixture and a condition check in `deriveHydrationState`, so the real app cannot detect or surface that recoverable stale case, and the pending/failed branches are only exercised through test doubles/fixtures rather than the runtime path.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:763:    planSummary: \"Replaced the fake immediate-refresh success path with a runtime-backed hydration executor that resolves a real process working-copy target, uses the provider adapter hydration/rehydration seam, returns pending/failed/not-available truthfully, and only marks a source hydrated after actual runtime refresh completion.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:806:      - \"Project-scoped refresh now requires exactly one current process working set to own the source attachment; if zero or multiple current processes reference it, the route returns SOURCE_ATTACHMENT_REFRESH_NOT_AVAILABLE because Story 3 does not define a cross-process hydration target.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:868:        evidence: \"The story/design says project-scoped actions appear in the project source section and Story 3 includes refresh/rehydration for `stale` and `not_hydrated` attachments. In the client, `renderSourceAttachmentSection()` renders refresh controls for any source whose hydration state is `stale` or `not_hydrated` regardless of scope (`apps/platform/client/features/projects/source-attachment-section.ts`). In the new runtime path, `RuntimeSourceHydrationExecutor.resolveTargetProcessId()` returns `null` for project-scoped attachments unless exactly one current process references the source, and `refreshRecoverableSource()` then returns `kind: 'not_available'` with message `no single current process working copy owns this source attachment` (`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts`). The modified API tests only verify successful refresh for process-scoped attachments; no server or client test proves the project-scoped case or suppresses the project-shell control when the backend will 409. This leaves project-scoped recoverability behavior unresolved and can present a false recovery path on the project shell.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:885:        evidence: \"The story/design says project-scoped actions appear in the project source section and Story 3 includes refresh/rehydration for `stale` and `not_hydrated` attachments. In the client, `renderSourceAttachmentSection()` renders refresh controls for any source whose hydration state is `stale` or `not_hydrated` regardless of scope (`apps/platform/client/features/projects/source-attachment-section.ts`). In the new runtime path, `RuntimeSourceHydrationExecutor.resolveTargetProcessId()` returns `null` for project-scoped attachments unless exactly one current process references the source, and `refreshRecoverableSource()` then returns `kind: 'not_available'` with message `no single current process working copy owns this source attachment` (`apps/platform/server/services/sources/source-refresh.service.ts`). The modified API tests only verify successful refresh for process-scoped attachments; no server or client test proves the project-scoped case or suppresses the project-shell control when the backend will 409. This leaves project-scoped recoverability behavior unresolved and can present a false recovery path on the project shell.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/03-hydration-and-freshness-management/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-007.md:921:      - \"Production refresh now has a new scope ambiguity for project-scoped attachments: the backend requires exactly one current owning process, while the project-shell UI still offers refresh purely from `stale`/`not_hydrated` state, so some project-scoped rows can advertise a recovery path that immediately returns `SOURCE_ATTACHMENT_REFRESH_NOT_AVAILABLE`.\"\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/stories/00-foundation.md:59:| Get process work surface | `GET` | `/api/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}` | Returns the process work surface, including environment summary and stable control states |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/00-foundation/sonnet-review.json:3:  \"result\": \"---\\n\\n## VERDICT: PASS\\n\\n**Gate:** `corepack pnpm run verify` — 176 tests across 29 files, all passing. Build clean.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Foundation checklist (30 items)\\n\\n28/30 **SATISFIED**, 1 **VIOLATED (minor)**, 1 **SATISFIED (inferred)**:\\n\\n- All contract vocabulary is in place: `environmentStateSchema` (10 states), `processSurfaceControlOrder` (7 controls, stable order), `buildProcessSurfaceControls` helper, `LastCheckpointResult`, `EnvironmentSummary`, `source.accessMode`, 3 new Epic 3 error codes, `environment` live entity schema, and `AppState.processSurface.environment`.\\n- All required fixtures exist: `process-environment.ts` (10 states), `checkpoint-results.ts` (5 types), `process-controls.ts` (stable order + `disabledReasons`), `materials.ts` (`accessMode` coverage), live fixtures for environment snapshot/upserts.\\n- The `environment` live apply branch in `process-live.ts` is wired and tested.\\n- The `process-work-surface.service.ts` returns `defaultEnvironmentSummary` as a correct Story 0 stub — the actual reader is scheduled for Story 1.\\n\\n**Single VIOLATED item:** No `environmentCompleteLiveFixture`. The DoD says \\\"completion markers\\\"; the schema permits `complete` for environment but no named fixture exercises it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Non-blocking warnings (3)\\n\\n1. **Silent default stub** — the service will return `absent` until Story 1 wires the `EnvironmentSectionReader`. No guard will catch a forgotten injection.\\n2. **Environment excluded from live error schema** — `liveProcessErrorMessageSchema` covers only `history | materials | side_work`. Environment errors travel through `environment.state`/`blockedReason`. Appears intentional but was not documented as a resolved decision.\\n3. **Route-level `controls` not asserted** — `process-work-surface-api.test.ts` covers `environment` but skips `controls`/`hasEnvironment` at the route layer; the foundation contract test covers this at service level.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### What I noticed but didn't report\\n\\n- `disabledReason` uses `.default(null)` not `.optional()` — stricter than the spec table but coherent.\\n- `environmentLiveDataMessageSchema` requires a full `EnvironmentSummary` payload even for `complete` messages — unlike `current_request` which allows `null`. Asymmetry that may matter when teardown needs a `complete` event.\\n- `reconnectingProcessSurfaceStateFixture.lastSequenceNumber = 17` — coherently tracks the highest sequence in the file.\",\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/04-durable-checkpoint-of-artifacts-and-writable-sources/red-phase-report.md:48:  - `returns a failed outcome with failureReason when canonical code persistence fails`\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/04-durable-checkpoint-of-artifacts-and-writable-sources/red-phase-report.md:104:- `returns a failed outcome with failureReason when canonical code persistence fails`\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/epic-review-sonnet.md:68:| TC-2.1a | SATISFIED | `process-work-surface-page.test.ts`: \"TC-2.1a and TC-2.5a clicking Start applies the returned process state without a manual refresh\" — shows `State: Preparing environment`; `process-actions-api.test.ts`: \"S2-TC-2.1a: start returns environment.state = preparing in the same session\" |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/epic-review-sonnet.md:69:| TC-2.1b | SATISFIED | `process-work-surface-page.test.ts`: \"TC-2.1b and TC-2.5b clicking Resume on a paused process applies the returned process state\"; `process-actions-api.test.ts`: \"S2-TC-2.1b: resume returns environment.state = preparing when environment work is required\" |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/codex-impl-log.md:277:  `DefaultProcessWorkSurfaceService` still returns `defaultEnvironmentSummary`\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/codex-impl-log.md:877:  `test:e2e` lane is scaffolded and intentionally returns `SKIP:` today, so the\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/codex-impl-log.md:1931:  teammate returns control to the orchestrator. Use direct Bash\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/codex-impl-log.md:2089:process (`codex exec`) gets orphaned/reaped when the teammate returns control\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/stories/02-start-or-resume-with-environment-preparation.md:115:`POST /api/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}/start` returns `200` with an updated `process` object, updated `environment` summary, and `currentRequest` when present. If environment preparation begins, the process surface updates in the same session and later environment progress continues through live updates.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/stories/02-start-or-resume-with-environment-preparation.md:119:`POST /api/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}/resume` returns `200` with an updated `process` object, updated `environment` summary, and `currentRequest` when present. If environment preparation begins, the process surface updates in the same session and later environment progress continues through live updates.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/stories/02-start-or-resume-with-environment-preparation.md:146:Once one of these actions returns success, later preparation, hydration, execution, recovery, or checkpoint failures surface as visible environment or checkpoint failure states in the same process surface session and on later reopen. Those later failures do not retroactively replace an already-accepted response with an HTTP error.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/01-environment-state-and-visible-controls/sonnet-convergence-01.md:36:The durable-state requirement covers more than bootstrap reads. If an action response returns `process.controls.start.enabled = true` (absent fallback) when the environment is actually `stale` (which should disable start), a client holding that response has incorrect control state until a full reload. That is same-session truth contradiction. My original confidence rating of HIGH for AC-1.4 was not earned.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/04-durable-checkpoint-of-artifacts-and-writable-sources/sonnet-review.md:25:| TC-4.3a | AC-4.3 | SATISFIED | `process-actions-api.test.ts` line 1150. `planFor` with `read_only` mode returns empty `codeTargets` and populated `skippedReadOnly`. Passes. OBSERVED. |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/04-durable-checkpoint-of-artifacts-and-writable-sources/sonnet-review.md:62:| `returns a failed outcome with failureReason when canonical code persistence fails` | `code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts` | YES (per green report) |\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/04-durable-checkpoint-of-artifacts-and-writable-sources/sonnet-review.md:96:OBSERVED. At line 218-228, `runCheckpointAsync` is called when `checkpointPlanner !== undefined || codeCheckpointWriter !== undefined || artifactCheckpointPersistence !== platformStore`. But at lines 256-261, `runCheckpointAsync` returns early if `checkpointPlanner === undefined || codeCheckpointWriter === undefined` (requires BOTH). This means the outer guard uses OR but the inner guard uses AND. In practice, `app.ts` wires both planner and writer together, so the discrepancy does not cause silent data loss in production. But a caller providing only `checkpointPlanner` would activate `runCheckpointAsync` at the outer gate and silently abort at the inner gate. MINOR defect — not blocking because no such caller exists in the current codebase and the production path wires both.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/04-durable-checkpoint-of-artifacts-and-writable-sources/sonnet-review.md:139:- OBSERVED: `InMemoryProviderAdapter.collectCheckpointCandidate` returns one artifact AND one code diff per call. This means in service-level tests that rely on `buildExecutionStore` (no source attachments), the fallback `read_write` default for the code diff's unknown `sourceAttachmentId` is active. The TC-4.1a test asserts `checkpointKind: 'artifact'` — it finds the intermediate per-artifact publication (emitted with `checkpointKind: 'artifact'` while still checkpointing). The final settled result would be `mixed`. The test passes but does not verify the final `lastCheckpointResult` kind. INFERRED minor weak assertion.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/03--process-environment-and-controlled-execution/story-verification/04-durable-checkpoint-of-artifacts-and-writable-sources/sonnet-review.md:141:- OBSERVED: `InMemoryPlatformStore.persistCheckpointArtifacts` correctly implements the seam and returns plausible output summaries for in-memory tests.\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/01-canonical-archive-entry-persistence/streams/014-verify.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/stories/01-canonical-archive-entry-persistence.md | sed -n '20,199p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    20\\t**Scope**\\n    21\\t\\n    22\\tIn:\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\t- `archiveEntries` Convex append/read functions\\n    25\\t- Atomic sequence assignment per process\\n    26\\t- `processId + finalizationKey` idempotency guard\\n    27\\t- Required archive entry kind validation\\n    28\\t- Rejection of unsupported archive entry kinds\\n    29\\t- Stable ascending read order using `sequence`\\n    30\\t- Same-timestamp deterministic ordering through sequence\\n    31\\t- Optional related artifact, source provenance, and tool correlation ids\\n    32\\t- Basic archive-entry page shape returned from store methods\\n    33\\t\\n    34\\tOut:\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\t- Live finalization hook behavior, owned by Story 2\\n    37\\t- Browser/user-facing archive read routes and UI, owned by Story 3\\n    38\\t- Read-time provenance enrichment, owned by Story 6\\n    39\\t- Turn and derived-view records\\n    40\\t- Historical migration/backfill\\n    41\\t\\n    42\\t**Dependencies**\\n    43\\t\\n    44\\t- Story 0 foundation\\n    45\\t- `convex/_generated/ai/guidelines.md` before Convex implementation\\n    46\\t- [tech-design.md](../tech-design.md)\\n    47\\t- [test-plan.md](../test-plan.md)\\n    48\\t\\n    49\\t### Acceptance Criteria\\n    50\\t<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\\n    51\\t**AC-1.1:** The canonical archive-entry primitive persists finalized low-level entries supplied by trusted callers.\\n    52\\t\\n    53\\t- **TC-1.1a: User message entry appended through primitive**\\n    54\\t  - Given: A trusted caller submits a finalized `user_message` payload for one process\\n    55\\t  - When: The archive-entry append primitive is invoked\\n    56\\t  - Then: A canonical `user_message` archive entry is persisted for that process\\n    57\\t- **TC-1.1b: Model message entry appended through primitive**\\n    58\\t  - Given: A trusted caller submits a finalized `model_message` payload for one process\\n    59\\t  - When: The archive-entry append primitive is invoked\\n    60\\t  - Then: A canonical `model_message` archive entry is persisted for that process\\n    61\\t- **TC-1.1c: Process event entry appended through primitive**\\n    62\\t  - Given: A trusted caller submits a finalized `process_event` payload for one process\\n    63\\t  - When: The archive-entry append primitive is invoked\\n    64\\t  - Then: A canonical `process_event` archive entry is persisted for that process\\n    65\\t\\n    66\\t**AC-1.2:** The canonical archive supports the PRD entry taxonomy.\\n    67\\t\\n    68\\t- **TC-1.2a: Required archive entry kinds accepted**\\n    69\\t  - Given: The platform appends finalized archive entries\\n    70\\t  - When: Each required entry kind is recorded\\n    71\\t  - Then: The archive accepts `user_message`, `model_message`, `reasoning`, `script_emission`, `tool_call`, `tool_result`, and `process_event`\\n    72\\t- **TC-1.2b: Unsupported archive entry kind rejected**\\n    73\\t  - Given: A caller attempts to append an unsupported archive entry kind\\n    74\\t  - When: The append request is validated\\n    75\\t  - Then: The platform rejects the entry and does not append a partial archive record\\n    76\\t\\n    77\\t**AC-1.3:** Archive entries preserve order within one process.\\n    78\\t\\n    79\\t- **TC-1.3a: Process archive entries read in stable order**\\n    80\\t  - Given: Multiple archive entries exist for one process\\n    81\\t  - When: The archive is read in ascending order\\n    82\\t  - Then: Entries appear in their canonical process order\\n    83\\t- **TC-1.3b: Same-timestamp entries remain deterministic**\\n    84\\t  - Given: Multiple archive entries have the same timestamp\\n    85\\t  - When: The archive is read\\n    86\\t  - Then: The platform returns them in a stable deterministic order\\n    87\\t\\n    88\\t**AC-1.4:** Archive entries can store and return related process, artifact, source, or tool ids without depending on read-time enrichment.\\n    89\\t\\n    90\\t- **TC-1.4a: Related ids round-trip through archive storage**\\n    91\\t  - Given: An archive entry includes related artifact, source provenance, or tool correlation ids\\n    92\\t  - When: The archive entry is appended and later read through the primitive\\n    93\\t  - Then: The stored related ids are returned unchanged on the canonical archive row\\n    94\\t- **TC-1.4b: Archive row remains readable without related-record lookup**\\n    95\\t  - Given: An archive entry stores nullable related ids and no related-record enrichment is performed\\n    96\\t  - When: The archive entry is read through the primitive\\n    97\\t  - Then: The canonical archive row remains readable without requiring artifact or source resolution\\n    98\\t\\n    99\\t### Technical Design\\n   100\\t<!-- Jira: Technical Notes or sub-section of Description -->\\n   101\\tThis story owns only the durable canonical archive-entry primitive.\\n   102\\t\\n   103\\t#### Architecture Context\\n   104\\t\\n   105\\tStory 1 creates the archive domain’s durable append/read primitive. Fastify and\\n   106\\tlater services decide when something is finalized enough to archive, but this\\n   107\\tstory owns the canonical row shape, per-process ordering, and idempotent append\\n   108\\tcontract that the rest of Epic 7 builds on.\\n   109\\t\\n   110\\t#### Convex Tables and Indexes\\n   111\\t\\n   112\\t| Table | Responsibility |\\n   113\\t|---|---|\\n   114\\t| `archiveEntries` | Canonical finalized low-level archive entries |\\n   115\\t\\n   116\\t| Index | Fields | Purpose |\\n   117\\t|---|---|---|\\n   118\\t| `by_processId_sequence` | `processId`, `sequence` | Canonical archive pagination and stable ordering |\\n   119\\t| `by_processId_finalizationKey` | `processId`, `finalizationKey` | Idempotency guard |\\n   120\\t| `by_projectId_processId_recordedAt` | `projectId`, `processId`, `recordedAt` | Project/process scoped diagnostics |\\n   121\\t\\n   122\\t#### PlatformStore Methods\\n   123\\t\\n   124\\t| Method | Description |\\n   125\\t|---|---|\\n   126\\t| `appendArchiveEntry(args)` | Appends one finalized canonical entry or returns/no-ops an existing entry for the same process/finalization key |\\n   127\\t| `listArchiveEntries(args)` | Returns one bounded page sorted by ascending `sequence` |\\n   128\\t\\n   129\\t#### Implementation Targets\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t| Area | Files / Modules |\\n   132\\t|------|-----------------|\\n   133\\t| Durable archive storage | `convex/archiveEntries.ts`, `convex/schema.ts` |\\n   134\\t| Store seam | `apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts` |\\n   135\\t| Shared archive contract consumption | `apps/platform/shared/contracts/archive.ts` |\\n   136\\t\\n   137\\tImplementation notes:\\n   138\\t\\n   139\\t- Convex assigns `sequence` inside the append mutation. Fastify may preflight finalization keys, but Convex owns the atomic guard.\\n   140\\t- The append mutation validates the seven-entry taxonomy and rejects unsupported kinds without persisting partial rows.\\n   141\\t- `lifecycleState` is always `finalized`.\\n   142\\t- `finalizationKey` is unique within a process and stable across retries.\\n   143\\t- Same-timestamp ordering is deterministic because reads sort by `sequence`, not `recordedAt`.\\n   144\\t- Related ids are nullable and round-trip as stored values on the canonical row.\\n   145\\t- Missing related records do not delete or hide the archive row.\\n   146\\t- Service-level finalization proof is completed in Story 2.\\n   147\\t- Full artifact/source enrichment and related-record lookup degradation are completed in Story 6.\\n   148\\t\\n   149\\t#### Design References\\n   150\\t\\n   151\\t- [tech-design.md](/Users/leemoore/code/liminal-build/docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/tech-design.md:139), lines 139-149\\n   152\\t- [tech-design.md](/Users/leemoore/code/liminal-build/docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/tech-design.md:223), lines 223-271\\n   153\\t- [tech-design.md](/Users/leemoore/code/liminal-build/docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/tech-design.md:563), lines 563-585\\n   154\\t- [test-plan.md](/Users/leemoore/code/liminal-build/docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/test-plan.md:36), lines 36-44\\n   155\\t\\n   156\\t#### Test Mapping\\n   157\\t\\n   158\\t| TC | Test File / Check | Test Description |\\n   159\\t|----|-------------------|------------------|\\n   160\\t| TC-1.1a | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | appends finalized `user_message` entry through primitive |\\n   161\\t| TC-1.1b | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | appends finalized `model_message` entry through primitive |\\n   162\\t| TC-1.1c | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | appends finalized `process_event` entry through primitive |\\n   163\\t| TC-1.2a | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | accepts required archive entry kinds |\\n   164\\t| TC-1.2b | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | rejects unsupported archive entry kind |\\n   165\\t| TC-1.3a | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | reads entries in stable sequence order |\\n   166\\t| TC-1.3b | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | same timestamp entries remain deterministic |\\n   167\\t| TC-1.4a | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | round-trips related artifact/source/tool ids |\\n   168\\t| TC-1.4b | `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts` | reads archive row without requiring related-record lookup |\\n   169\\t\\n   170\\t#### Non-TC Decided Tests\\n   171\\t\\n   172\\t- `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts`: sequence assignment is atomic across same-process appends\\n   173\\t- `convex/archiveEntries.test.ts`: same `processId + finalizationKey` returns or no-ops an existing archive row\\n   174\\t\\n   175\\t#### Technical Notes\\n   176\\t\\n   177\\t- This story owns durable append/read primitives only. It does not decide when an object is final enough to append, and it does not prove service-level finalization hooks or read-time provenance enrichment.\\n   178\\t\\n   179\\t#### Anti-Shim Requirements\\n   180\\t\\n   181\\t- Prove stable order and idempotency through the real persistence boundary, not by sorting mock arrays in a unit helper.\\n   182\\t\\n   183\\t#### Verification\\n   184\\t\\n   185\\t- Targeted: `pnpm run test:convex`\\n   186\\t- Story gate: `pnpm run green-verify`\\n   187\\t- Epic gate: `pnpm run verify-all`\\n   188\\t\\n   189\\t#### Spec Deviations\\n   190\\t\\n   191\\tNone.\\n   192\\t\\n   193\\tSee the tech design document for full architecture, implementation targets, and test mapping.\\n   194\\t\\n   195\\t### Definition of Done\\n   196\\t<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\n   197\\t- `archiveEntries` durable functions append and read finalized entries\\n   198\\t- All required archive entry kinds are accepted; unsupported kinds are rejected without partial persistence\\n   199\\t- Sequence assignment is stable, per-process, and deterministic for same-timestamp entries\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/01-canonical-archive-entry-persistence/streams/014-verify.stdout.log:17:{\"type\